# Hēti Project Rules

- All project structure, development, and reasoning must follow these rules.
- You can @ files, @folders, or @git references for precision.
- Markdown is allowed, but not required.

# Hēti Project Rules
See @file(HETI_PROJECT_OVERVIEW.mdc) for project purpose, expectations, and background.

---

## 1. @project/modularity – Modular Code Only
All code must be organized in explicit, self-contained modules:
- agent/
- tools/
- ui/
- memory/
- perception/
- config/
- logs/
New files must include a docstring stating their purpose and integration points.

---

## 2. @.cursor/specs – No Code Without Spec
No file or function is to be generated, changed, or reviewed without a clear, written mini-spec covering:
- Purpose
- Inputs/outputs
- Tech stack
- Limits/behavior
Specs live in `.cursor/` or as docstrings.

---

## 3. @tests – Test-First Development
Tests must be written (or locked) before code generation.
- All new modules require unit and integration tests.
- Code is only “done” when all relevant tests pass.

---

## 4. @project/atomicity – Single-Focus Workflow
Work is always done one file, class, or function at a time.
- Each commit covers only one change.
- No big merges or cross-cutting edits.

---

## 5. @logs – Full Traceability
Every output, state change, and error must be logged with:
- Timestamp
- Module
- Summary of input/output
- Reasoning/confidence (if applicable)
All edits and failures must be reviewable. Lessons learned go in `.cursor/failure_cases.md`.

---

## 6. @agent/permission_escalation.py – Safety on All Sensitive Actions
All critical actions (delete, write, API, system-level) require explicit user permission via UI, voice, or key.
- No silent destructive behavior.
- All escalation events must be logged.

---

## 7. @config/context – Adaptive, Context-Aware Behavior
Agent must always respect context (`mode_lock.yaml`, `autonomy_levels.yaml`).
- On mode or context change, agent adapts instantly.
- Behavior and output must match user’s current state.

---

## 8. @agent/explainability_log.py – Reasoning is Always Logged
Every significant decision/action must be accompanied by a natural-language explanation.
- Logs must be queryable and linked to the triggering test or context.

---

## 9. @agent/self_review.py – Continuous Self-Improvement
Agent must self-review regularly (at least weekly):
- Summarize failures, inefficiencies, and corrections.
- Adapt persona and knowledge from real usage data.
- Improvements logged and tracked in `.cursor/`.

---

## 10. @agent/goal_manager.py – Strict User Alignment
No action may occur unless it aligns to a specific user goal, config, or memory state.
- All ambiguous or unaligned instructions must be clarified or deferred.
- User intent is always prioritized over default or autonomous behavior.

---

